{
  "id": "Compound::DB06201 - Disease::DOID:1826",
  "verbalization": "Rufinamide is implicated in the treatment of Epilepsy Syndrome through multiple indirect pathways, all of which share a common mechanism of action involving anticonvulsant agents. The evidence suggests that Rufinamide may contribute to the development of Epilepsy Syndrome through the induction of head injury, iron deficiency anaemia, or status epilepticus, which in turn are treated by Levetiracetam, Pregabalin, or Zonisamide, respectively. These anticonvulsants are classified as organic heterocyclic compounds and are known to have antiepileptic properties. The shared classification as anticonvulsants and the presence of organic heterocyclic compounds in the LCA of Rufinamide and its downstream targets suggest a potential link between Rufinamide and Epilepsy Syndrome through a chain of anticonvulsant effects. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of anticonvulsants in the pathophysiology of epilepsy, with Rufinamide potentially acting as a precursor or mediator in the development of the syndrome.",
